the 22-year rule of president ferdinand  marcus is seen today as a brutal and  divisive period in philippine history  a people power revolution forced him out  of office and into exile in 1986  but decades later his son ferdinand  marcus jr also known as bongbong is a  front-runner in this year's presidential  election my intention to run it may seem  surprising to many but the political  resurgence of the marcuses didn't happen  in an instant  experts say the marcuses have spent  years using social media to rewrite  history  online memes like this  new zealand prime minister jacinta adern  praising bonbon marcus jr  or of the american basketball superstar  michael jordan receiving a box of bread  from the late president have dominated  online chat groups  it's even said that the marcuses will  give away tons of gold bars and other  treasure if bongbong becomes president  treasure that fact checkers and  journalists say does not exist  but the story has already spread widely  on social media even after twitter  announced last week that it has  suspended hundreds of pro-marcus  accounts i think it's also important to  consider the desperation of  many poor filipinos  and that desperation economic and social  their needs  the pandemic has exacerbated that and  this is just demarco says exploiting  that desperation  making filipinos believe that there is a  quick way out of poverty al jazeera  requested an interview with mr marcus's  campaign team  there was no response  the marcus camp has repeatedly denied  owning social media troll farms  we know that social media has very low  verifiability the philippines once a  shining star on democracy in asia is now  called the backsliding country  the philippines is the biggest facebook  user in southeast asia and the sixth in  the world researchers say the higher  populations exposure to social media the  higher its vulnerability to  disinformation this information they say  has the power to obliterate the ability  of people to distinguish what is real  and what is not jemela allendogen al  jazeera manila
 

